<div class="topline"><a href="/websites">WEBSITE MANAGER</a><span>Connect once. Manage declared capabilities.</span></div><header class="page-header"><div><h1>Connect a website you own.</h1><p class="muted">The panel is generated from the website's fields and permissions. A domain or server IP supplies its address; an authorized API supplies access.</p></div></header>
<section class="card"><h2>Provider gateway for client websites</h2><p>Host one gateway under your agency account. Give each client a gateway address and client username/password, then assign their websites. Hosting credentials and website connector tokens stay with your agency.</p><p>Clients use one provider sign-in to open all assigned sites. WordPress, PHP, Next.js and other sites use an adapter behind the gateway; public content sites can save directly in its database.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn" href="/websites#provider-login">Sign in with a provider</a><a class="btn secondary" href="/websites/gateway/download" hx-boost="false">Download provider gateway</a></div><p class="footnote">The agency deploys the gateway once. Content-mode edits do not require GitHub Actions or a website rebuild.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Choose your website backend</h2><p class="muted">Mix different technologies in the same workspace. Each website connects through its own authorized adapter.</p><div class="website-adapter-grid"><div><h3>PHP</h3><p>Website accounts and editable content collections on PHP hosting.</p><code>/api/lifeos-admin.php</code><a class="btn secondary" href="/websites/connector/download" hx-boost="false">Download PHP connector</a></div><div><h3>WordPress</h3><p>Manage native pages and posts. Sign in with a WordPress Application Password.</p><code>/wp-json/lifeos/v1/admin</code><a class="btn secondary" href="/websites/adapters/wordpress/download" hx-boost="false">Download WordPress plugin</a></div><div><h3>Next.js / Node.js</h3><p>Connect existing models or a versioned content store. The included example uses Advanced API-token sign-in.</p><code>/api/lifeos-admin</code><a class="btn secondary" href="/websites/adapters/nextjs/download" hx-boost="false">Download Next.js adapter</a></div></div><p class="footnote">Laravel, Django, Java and other backends can implement the same API contract. A static website needs an authenticated backend before it can expose admin actions. Each package includes deployment instructions and supported capabilities.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>How the connection works</h2><ol><li>Add the website URL, optional business name, server details and technology in LifeOS.</li><li>Install the matching PHP, WordPress or Next.js adapter, or implement the same API in your backend.</li><li>Configure the adapter on your server. Keep credentials private; the PHP package creates website accounts, WordPress uses Application Passwords, and the Next.js example uses a server-side API token.</li><li>Enter the HTTPS connector endpoint in LifeOS, then use the sign-in method described by that adapter. Its declared collections generate the forms and record views.</li><li>Repeat for all your websites. Switch between them from All websites or the website switcher. Each website keeps its own session and permissions.</li><li>Load records, review changes, and save to that website. Sign out when finished.</li></ol><a class="btn secondary" href="/websites/connector/download" hx-boost="false">Download connector &amp; protocol guide</a><p class="footnote">The reference connector has its own content store and a public read helper. Your public website must read that content, or its adapter must write to the website's existing backend. Deploying a connector alone does not rewrite an existing site.</p></section>
<section class="card" id="references"><h2>Your admin-panel references</h2><div class="two-col"><div><h3>CreativeFX · content studio</h3><p>Homepage sections, projects, weddings, services, about, creative pool, media library, navigation/footer, contact, SEO, branding, analytics and admin settings.</p><a href="/websites/references/creativefx/download" hx-boost="false">Download reference source snapshot</a></div><div><h3>TenderHub · operations console</h3><p>Grouped administration, tenders, organizations, staff, subscription/payment workflows and integration operations. Its role and tenant rules belong to the TenderHub backend.</p><a href="/websites/references/tenderhub/download" hx-boost="false">Download reference source snapshot</a></div></div><p class="muted">The original projects are preserved. These source snapshots document their complete panel concepts for adaptation. They are not installed into either production website.</p><p class="footnote">A working clone of their publishing, uploads, permissions, payments or automation needs the corresponding APIs, authentication and data mapping. The universal collection editor provides the actions declared by a connected backend; it does not impersonate those project-specific engines.</p></section>
